rn 'null'\n47:   ...\n50: }\n51: (blank)\n52: function matchesType(type, value) {\n```\nAfter deleting lines 45-51 (7 lines), line 52 → line 45. Yes, that matches. So current line 45 = matchesType. Consistent.\n\nOK. But hold on — I should verify the \"only 7 lines removed and nothing else changed\" claim. I can do this by reading the git blob of the base version from `.git/objects`. But that requires git object decompression (zlib). The precedent (task 001, 006 verdicts) did this: \"只读解压 .git/objects/... 并重算 SHA-1\". I can do that with Node's zlib in a read-only way (just reading files, no writing). That's allowed — it's read-only. Let me consider whether it's worth doing. It would strengthen the verification of A-19 (only typeOf removed) and A-06/A-07/A-11/A-27 (no diff to certain files). Actually I can't easily do git diff without git, but I can compare the HEAD tree blob with the worktree file. Hmm, but the HEAD is the dev's final commit which already includes all changes. To compare with base f6f6057, I need to read the base tree. \n\nActually, a simpler approach for most \"unchanged\" checks: compare the worktree file with the blob in the HEAD commit (which equals the worktree if committed) AND with the base commit blob f6f6057. If both equal → unchanged. I can do this with a Node script reading .git objects using zlib. That's read-only and doesn't execute git commands. Let me think about whether this is necessary.\n\nActually, the acceptance items A-06, A-07, A-11, A-19, A-27 involve `git diff f6f6057 -- <file>`. These are \"execution类\" items that CI ran and passed. Per the instruction, I can cite CI results for those. But the instruction also says \"其余请用只读方式（读文件、算哈希、核对行号）独立验证实现与规格是否一致\" — for the rest, use read-only to verify. And \"独立判断是否真的满足（不要只看文件存在）\". So I should do some independent verification. Let me do a reasonable amount: verify key implementation details by reading code (done), verify hashes of the [repro] sections, verify the sha chain, verify write scope by mtime, etc.\n\nLet me think harder about whether there's a planted defect. So far the implementation looks quite clean. Let me look at the check-unused.mjs scanner more carefully for a subtle bug.\n\nPotential issues in check-unused.mjs:\n\n1. design §4.1: \"不带 path 时扫描 lib\". Implementation: `list = Array.isArray(paths) && paths.length > 0 ? paths : ['lib']`. ✓\n2. \"path 相对 --root（默认仓库根）\". Implementation: `full = path.resolve(base, rel)`. ✓\n3. \"目录递归，只取 .mjs/.js/.cjs，按码点排序\". Implementation: collectFiles recurses, SOURCE_EXT test, `.sort(byCodePoint)`. But wait — `found.sort((a,b) => byCodePoint(a.rel, b.rel))` sorts by rel path globally. ✓\n4. Text output: `<posix 相对路径>:<行号> unused-import <名字>` or `unused-function <名字>`. Implementation: `${item.file}:${item.line} unused-${item.kind} ${item.name}` → \"unused-import\" / \"unused-function\". ✓\n5. Last line: `check-unused: ok (files=N)` or `check-unused: FAIL (N unused)`. ✓\n6. `--json`: stdout only one JSON. Implementation: writes JSON + newline, exitCode 0 or 1. ✓\n7. Exit codes: 0/1/2. Unknown args → usageError sets exitCode 2. Path not exists → scanPaths throws ENOENT → usageError → 2. ✓\n8. \"只在作为入口运行时执行 main，被 import 时无副作用\". Implementation: `invokedDirectly` check with path.resolve comparison. ✓\n9. Exports scanSource, scanPaths. ✓\n\nScanning rules:\n1. Preprocessing: remove // and /* */ comments, single/double quoted strings, template text; template ${...} expressions preserved (nested supported). Regex literal detection by previous token. Implementation maskSource does this. Let me check nested template ${} handling: stack of frames. When in template and `ch === '$' && text[i+1] === '{'`, push `{ kind: 'code', expr: true, depth: 0 }`. Then in code frame with expr=true, `{` increments depth, `}` at depth 0 pops. That handles nested braces. But what about nested template literals inside ${}? When it sees a backtick in code frame, it pushes template frame. ✓ Good.\n2. Unused imports: top-level import declarations (can span lines), each local binding — default, named (a as b → b), namespace (* as ns). Except import declaration itself, identifier appears 0 times → report. Side-effect import `import 'x'`, dynamic `import(…)`, `import.meta` not reported. Implementation: collectImports. Let me check:\n   - `if (token.value !== 'import' || token.depth !== 0) continue`. \n   - `next && (next.value === '(' || next.value === '.')` → skip (dynamic import, import.meta). ✓\n   - if next non-ws char is quote → side effect import, scope.add(k), continue. ✓ (adds the `import` token to scope so no binding)\n   - find `from` at depth 0. ✓\n   - scope: `for (let j = k; j <= fromIndex; j += 1) scope.add(j)` — marks all tokens from import to from (inclusive) as declaration scope. But what about the module specifier string after `from`? It's a string, masked to spaces, so no word tokens. OK. But what about `;` after? Not in scope. Fine.\n   - bindings: iterates tokens k+1..fromIndex-1, skips `'as'`, handles `x as y` → pushes y. For default import `fs` → pushes fs. For `import { readFileSync, existsSync }` → pushes readFileSync, existsSync. For `import * as os` → tokens: `*`, `as`, `os` → `after` for `*` is `as` word → local = tokens[j+2] = `os` → push os. ✓ For `import { join as joinPath }` → join then `as` → local = joinPath → push. ✓\n   \n   Wait, there's a subtle bug: the scope set contains token indices from `k` (the `import` word) to `fromIndex` (the `from` word), inclusive. But bindings are collected from those same tokens. So when checking occurrences, `hits = occurrences.get(binding.value).filter(index => !scope.has(index))`. The binding's own token index is in scope → excluded. ✓ Good.\n   \n   Hmm, but what about a named import used as a shorthand in an object, like `{ helper }`? The word `helper` in the object literal would be counted as an occurrence (design §4.2 rule 4: \"对象字面量的键 `{ a: 1 }` 无法可靠区分，按引用计\" — object literal keys count as references). So `{ helper }` counts as a reference. ✓ per design.\n\n3. Unused top-level functions: brace depth 0 `function name`, `async function name`, `function* name` declarations, no `export` before. Except declaration name and its own body, identifier appears 0 times → report (self-recursive counts as unused). `export function`, `export default function` not reported. Implementation collectFunctions:\n   - `token.value !== 'function' || token.depth !== 0` → skip.\n   - `before`: walk back over `async` / `default` words. Then `leading = tokens[before]`. If `export` → skip. If `.` → skip (method).\n   \n   Hmm wait: for `export default function foo() {}`, tokens: `export`, `default`, `function`, `foo`, ... `before` starts at k-1 = `default` (word), walks back → `export` (word, but not async/default → loop stops). Actually the while loop condition: `tokens[before].type === 'word' && (value === 'async' || value === 'default')`. So from `default`, before becomes index of `export`, which is word but value 'export' → not async/default → loop stops. `leading = tokens[before]` = `export` → skip. ✓\n   \n   For `export async function foo()` → tokens `export`, `async`, `function`. before from `async` → `export` → leading = export → skip. ✓\n   \n   For plain `async function foo()` → before = async, then before-1 = previous token (maybe `;` or `}`), loop stops, leading = that char → not export, not '.' → OK, analyzed. ✓\n   \n   - nameIndex: k+1, skip `*`. name must be word. ✓\n   - open: find `(` after name. closeParen via matchPair. bodyOpen: find `{` after closeParen. bodyClose via matchPair. ✓\n   - found.push({name, nameIndex, bodyOpen, bodyClose}).\n   \n   In scanSource: `hits = occurrences.get(fn.name).filter(index => index !== fn.nameIndex && !(index > fn.bodyOpen && index < fn.bodyClose))`. So name token excluded, and any occurrence strictly inside body (bodyOpen < index < bodyClose) excluded. Self-recursion inside body → excluded → unused. ✓\n   \n   Hmm — but note: `index > fn.bodyOpen