{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b827-dd49-7cce-8c4e-4b0999a36e1b","session_id":"01a1b824-ffca-72a9-8d1d-a82a9e9bca00","message":{"id":"01a1b827dd497cce8c4e4b0739bd62e0","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. So the human input #2 explicitly requested the acceptance.md rewrite. So the rewrite was authorized. Good — no integrity issue there. Though note: the dev was told \"Rewrite the whole acceptance.md in that shape. Do not patch it line by line.\" It's a \"note\", which the dev consumed. OK.\n\nNow let me verify the [repro] hashes. I need to compute the SHA-256 of the [repro] sections. The check-defects.mjs uses a specific extraction: \"块 = 行首 test('[repro] 且以 { 结尾 → 其后第一个行首 '})'，CRLF→LF，'\\n' 连接\" (from task 002 verdict). Let me read check-defects.mjs to get the exact repro extraction algorithm, then compute the hashes with a read-only Node script.\n\nActually, I can just run `node scripts/check-defects.mjs` — wait, would that be considered \"executing git commands\"? check-defects.mjs probably uses git to get file contents? Let me check. Actually in task 006 review, the reviewer ran `node scripts/check-defects.mjs` and got 'check-defects: ok'. So it's a read-only script that doesn't run git. Hmm, but it might spawn child processes for canary repro tests. Let me check.\n\nActually, let me be careful about the prohibition: \"禁止：修改 ... 被评审的代码与文档；执行 git 命令；创建其它文件\". Running a read-only node script that reads files is fine. But scripts that create temp files... hmm, \"评审环境可能缺 pwsh 7、或缺临时文件权限——这不能作为判 needs_revision 的理由\" — the environment may lack temp file permissions. Running check-defects.mjs might create temp dirs (canary). But those are outside the repo, in os.tmpdir(). That's fine — not creating files in the repo. Actually \"创建其它文件\" likely refers to files in the repo/task dir. Hmm, ambiguous. To be safe, let me avoid running scripts that write files. I can replicate the hash computation in a read-only way with a Node one-liner that only reads files. Actually simplest: write a node -e script that reads the three defect files, extracts the [repro] block per the algorithm, normalizes CRLF→LF, computes sha256, prints. No file writes. That's pure read-only.\n\nLet me first read check-defects.mjs to get the exact extractReproSection algorithm.\n\nActually, A-20 (check-defects: ok) is execution evidence from CI. And design §1.1 point 6 says \"[repro] 块一字不改\". I can verify by computing hashes. Let me read check-defects.mjs.\n\nAlso let me check the golden quota-shapes.json count and the baseline v0.1 select.mjs for the count clamp question. Let me batch these reads.\n\nActually, let me focus on finding the real defect. Let me think about what could be wrong.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me re-examine `selectModels` in lib/select.mjs regarding the `usage` parameter. design §2.2: `selectModels(config, request = {}, { status = {}, usage = [], ctx } = {})`. The third param has `usage = []` which is the usage records array. And `request.usage` is the normalized usage kind (Heavy/Normal/Light). OK.\n\nNow: `const records = Array.isArray(usage) ? usage : []`. ✓\n\nLet me check `normalizePrices` from forecast.mjs — used in listModels. And `billedTokens` from pools.mjs.\n\nNow let me think about a potential issue with `draftHandle` and the `prices` field: design §3 says handle keys include `prices` which is `m.prices`. listModels sets `prices: normalizePrices(m)`. ✓\n\nAnd `quota`: from forecastOf entry. ✓\n\n`adapter`: `config.providers?.[m.provider]?.adapter ?? null`. ✓\n\nOK. Let me now think about the A-09 test (golden Heavy rejects). The acceptance A-09 command builds cases from quota-shapes.json filter `c.expected.risk`. Then for each case, provider p{i}, model 'm-'+c.id, quotas[p{i}] = c.input.quota. ctx.forecast = buildForecast({quota: a.quota, samples: byKey[a.model].input.samples, usage: a.usage, now: 0}). Then Heavy: want = cases with risk in bad list. got = rejected with reason E_FORECAST_EXHAUSTION. Assert got === want, want.length >= 5, h.rejected.length === want.length, Normal/Light rejected.length === 0.\n\nHmm — there's a subtlety: `models['m-'+c.id] = { provider: 'p'+i, id: 'x'+i, level: 'Quick', identity: 'verified' }`. No `prices` field. normalizePrices(m) presumably returns defaults. Let me check forecast.mjs normalizePrices. Also, buildForecast might need `prices`? Let's see: buildForecast({quota, samples, usage, now}) — no prices passed. OK.\n\nLet me now consider: is there a scenario where a model with risk 'exhausted' under Heavy is NOT rejected because of the prefilter? The prefilter checks exclude, allowed, level, callable, identity, readonly. All models are Quick, callable (status set for all providers), identity verified. `requireReadonly` defaults false. So all pass. Then heavyRejection. ✓\n\nOK. So A-09 should pass. And CI says it did.\n\nLet me look for the defect in a different area. Let me check the `check-seams.mjs` expectation: A-22 expects `files=2[12]`. design §7 point 2: select.mjs and select-draft.mjs must not have bare Date.now, zero-arg new Date(), global timers. Implementation: select.mjs imports systemNow from context.mjs. select-draft.mjs has no imports. ✓\n\nHmm. What about `check-no-collab.mjs`? A-23 expects `check-no-collab: ok` prefix. New files must not reference .collab. Let me check: select.mjs, select-draft.mjs, check-unused.mjs, fixtures, tests, traceability. Do any contain \".collab\"? Let me grep. Actually the [fixed] tests use `pathToFileURL`, `fileURLToPath`, no .collab. The test ROOT uses `new URL('../../', import.meta.url)`. check-unused.mjs uses REPO_ROOT = path.resolve(HERE, '..'). No .collab literal. ✓ Let me verify with a grep later.\n\nWhat about `check-golden-frozen.mjs` (14 files)? A-23 expects `check-golden-frozen: ok (14 files)`. Could the dev have modified a golden file? Probably not. Let me verify by checking file list / mtime.\n\nWhat about `check-traceability.mjs` → A-21 expects `covered=22 planned=52`. The base was covered=19 planned=55. Adding T-007.json with 3 covered ACs → 22/52. ✓ Let me verify T-007.json content matches design §6.4. ✓ It does.\n\nHmm. What about A-26 `npm test` → `fail 0`, `run-tests: full 34 files`. CI says pass. \n\nWhat about A-27 package.json dependencies 0 and no diff. CI says pass.\n\nLet me now consider the write_scope issue more carefully. A-24: check-write-scope with --base f6f6057 and --scope-from design.md. The dev weakened the assertion. But actually, let me check: did the dev modify files OUTSIDE write_scope (in lib/scripts/test)? Let me check mtimes and compare with the base commit blobs. If there's an out-of-scope modification that A-24's weakened assertion masks (because it only checks FAIL lines start with .collab/), that would be a real finding!\n\nWait — A-24's command: `$bad = @($o | Where-Object { $_ -match '^FAIL' -and $_ -notmatch '^FAIL write_scope\\.outside \\.collab/' }); if ($bad.Count -ne 0) { $bad; exit 1 }`. So it fails if ANY FAIL line doesn't start with `FAIL write_scope.outside .collab/`. So a FAIL line like `FAIL write_scope.outside lib/foo.mjs` would cause exit 1. So code paths outside .collab/ are still checked. OK so A-24 still catches out-of-scope lib/scripts/test changes. Fine.\n\nBut hmm — what if the FAIL line format for a code path is different, e.g., `FAIL write_scope.modified lib/...`? Then `-notmatch '^FAIL write_scope\\.outside \\.collab/'` would be true → caught. So it still catches. OK.\n\nActually, wait. There's a subtle issue: A-24's first assertion `if (-not (\"$o\" -match 'check-write-scope: (ok|FAILED)')) { exit 1 }`. And then filters FAIL lines. But if there are no FAIL lines at all, `$bad.Count -eq 0` → pass. Fine.\n\nHmm, so A-24 is actually reasonably strong. The dev's \"relaxation\" is about the .collab/ scheduler files which are baseline noise. OK.\n\nBut hold on — let me reconsider. The design.md §0 says \"人类已裁决（方案 A）\" about write scope for validate.mjs. And A-24 uses `--scope-from design.md`, which reads the front matter write_scope — which INCLUDES `lib/contract/validate.mjs` (design.md front matter line 5 includes it). Wait, let me check: design.md front matter write_scope:\n```\nwrite_scope:\n  - lib/select.mjs\n  - lib/select-draft.mjs\n  - lib/contract/validate.mjs\n  - scripts/check-unused.mjs\n  - test/fixtures/unused/**\n  - test/unit/select-draft.test.mjs\n  - test/unit/check-unused.test.mjs\n  - test/characterization/defects/a5-dup-call.test.mjs\n  - test/characterization/defects/a5-forecast-x3.test.mjs\n  - test/characterization/defects/a5-unused.test.mjs\n  - test/traceability/T-007.json\n```\nYes, includes validate.mjs. Good, so the typeOf deletion is within scope.\n\nBut brief.md's write_scope does NOT include validate.mjs. Hmm. But the human input explicitly added it: \"lib/contract/validate.mjs added to T-007 write_scope (main and worktree)\". And design.md front matter includes it. So A-24 uses design.md's scope. Fine.\n\nOK. So where is the defect? Let me think about whether this task is actually a \"pass\" task. Maybe it IS a pass task and I should just do thorough verification and conclude pass. The earlier tasks: 001 pass (r1), 002 needs_revision, 003 many rounds, 004 pass, 006 pass. So pass is common. But the review instruction emphasizes \"独立判断是否真的满足（不要只看文件存在）\" and \"发现问题就判 needs_revision\" — this suggests I should look hard.\n\nLet me think about the deviations in impl.md §3 again:\n- §3.1 count not clamped to 10 (deviation from design §2.1 \"保持不变\"). \n- §3.2 file relative to scanned path instead of root (deviation from design §4.1).\n- §3.3 spread operator handling (refinement of design §4.2 rule 4).\n\nThese three are documented deviations. A reviewer might flag §3.1 as a genuine spec violation: design §2.1 explicitly says \"count 钳制到 1..10\" must be preserved. The dev removed the upper clamp. Is that acceptable? Let me check the baseline select.mjs to see if it actually clamped.\n\nLet me read the v0.1 copy of select.mjs and the base commit