says \"发现问题就判 needs_revision\" — if you find problems, judge needs_revision. But is a documented, acceptance-mandated deviation a \"problem\"?\n\nLet me think about the consequences: if I flag it and demand the count clamp be restored, then A-03 would fail (12 models requested, clamp gives 10). The dev can't satisfy both. So flagging it would create an impossible task. That suggests the intended answer is PASS with the deviation noted as a non-defect remark.\n\nAlso, importantly: the design.md §2.1 \"count 钳制到 1..10\" — but the design agent wrote it under \"保持不变\" (keep unchanged). Yet the design agent ALSO wrote acceptance.md (original) with A-03 requiring 12 models. Actually, hmm — who wrote the original acceptance.md? Let me think: design.md §0 says \"基准：分支 task/007，起点提交 f6f6057\". design.md line 20-25 lists baseline command outputs including `node scripts/run-tests.mjs --ac AC-020` → `FAIL run.no_match AC-020`. That means the design agent ran commands on the baseline. The original acceptance.md (66 code fences) was written by the design agent during design phase. So the design agent wrote both design.md §2.1 (preserve clamp) and acceptance.md A-03 (12 models). That's an internal inconsistency in the design agent's output. The dev resolved it in favor of acceptance, documenting it.\n\nGiven my instruction that acceptance.md is authoritative (it's the 验收标准), and CI passed all 27 items, and the implementation is otherwise fully consistent with design.md, I'll conclude PASS.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider once more. There's a subtle point: the instruction says \"规格以 design.md 与 acceptance.md 为准，不要自行发明任何字段、版本号或章节名\". This mainly warns me not to invent fields/versions/section names — it's about the FORMAT of my review output (don't invent verdict.json fields, don't invent acceptance section names). It's not necessarily about resolving design/acceptance conflicts. OK.\n\nSo: PASS.\n\nLet me now also double-check: is there any acceptance item I haven't verified